================================================================================================
BIFURCATION ORDER vs POSITION ON THE LANDSCAPE, at FIXED gain
================================================================================================
  gain = 0.54 (V6 default alpha*r0), R = 0.6 held.
  period-doubling <=> an eigenvalue of J crosses -1.
  'unstable' counts eigenvalues with Re(lambda) < -1.

  shallow  N=2 reps=1   M=8
    height band        n    mean|g|  mean unstable  mean max|lam|
    -------------------------------------------------------------
    low  (<0.4)       15     0.5156           0.13          1.647
    high (>=0.6)      14     0.5444           0.00          2.096
    all samples: mean|g| 0.5735   mean unstable 0.05   mean max|lam| 1.830

  deeper   N=2 reps=3: M=16 too wide for exact enumeration, skipped
  deep     N=3 reps=4  (plateau-ward): M=30 too wide for exact enumeration, skipped
================================================================================================
READING IT
================================================================================================
  The hypothesis predicts THREE things, and each can fail separately:
    1. within a circuit, 'unstable' rises from the low to the high band;
    2. across circuits, |g| falls as depth grows (approaching plateau);
    3. and max|lambda| RISES as |g| falls, because normalisation divides
       by a vanishing maximum - the plateau is the least stable regime,
       not the most.
  If (1) fails the landscape does not set the control parameter and the
  bifurcation is a step-size artefact, as v93 assumes. If (3) fails in
  the other direction - plateau most stable - then normalisation is
  damping rather than amplifying and the diagnostic reads backwards.
